### What is the problem?

Find All Anagrams in a String — Fixed-Size Window With Frequency Comparison. Find All Anagrams in a String — fixed-size window with frequency comparison.

### How does the approach work?

Build a frequency array `need` of size 26 for the pattern. Slide a window of width equal to the pattern length over the text, keeping a `window` frequency array. For each new right index add its character to `window`, and once the window exceeds the pattern length, remove the leftmost character. Whenever the window width equals the pattern length, compare the two arrays; if equal, record the start index.

A faster variant uses a single `matches` counter instead of comparing 26 cells each step. It counts how many of the 26 letters currently have equal frequency in the window and the pattern, updating `matches` incrementally as characters enter and leave, and reports a hit when `matches == 26`. A code snippet of the simpler array-compare version: `for (int r=0; r<text.length(); r++) { window[text.charAt(r)-'a']++; if (r >= pLen) window[text.charAt(r-pLen)-'a']--; if (r >= pLen-1 && Arrays.equals(window, need)) result.add(r - pLen + 1); }`.

The two methods trade clarity for speed. Comparing arrays each step is O(26) per position, which is O(26n) overall, still linear in n with a constant factor. The matched-count version removes that constant factor and is the senior-level answer, but both are acceptable and the array-compare version is easier to get right under pressure.

A senior nuance is the off-by-one on when to start comparing: you only compare once the window has reached the full pattern width, which is at right index pattern-length minus one and every index after. Also remember to shrink the window by removing the character that falls off the left as the window advances, otherwise the window grows unbounded and the counts drift.

### What is the banking use case?

Problem 1 — The National Bank of India fraud team has a known suspicious character pattern and must find every position in a long transaction-reference string where a same-length window is an anagram of it, since fraud rings sometimes permute a code. This is exactly how find-all-anagrams works — a fixed window compared by character frequency.

Problem 2 — The reference string is long, so an O(n) sliding comparison is required rather than checking every substring independently.

The code shows an `AnagramScanner` with `findAnagramStarts`, a demo on a reference string and a pattern, and the list of start indexes.

### How do you implement this in Java?

```java
// ─── File: src/main/java/com/bank/fraud/FindAnagramsDemo.java ───
package com.bank.fraud;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class FindAnagramsDemo {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(FindAnagramsDemo.class.getName());
    static {
        Logger root = Logger.getLogger("");
        for (Handler h : root.getHandlers()) root.removeHandler(h);
        ConsoleHandler ch = new ConsoleHandler();
        ch.setFormatter(new BankLogFormatter());
        root.addHandler(ch);
        root.setLevel(Level.ALL);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AnagramScanner scanner = new AnagramScanner();

        String text = "cbaebabacd";
        String pattern = "abc";
        List<Integer> hits = scanner.findAnagramStarts(text, pattern);
        log.info(() -> "Anagram start indexes of '" + pattern + "' in '" + text + "': " + hits);
    }
}

// ─── Would be in AnagramScanner.java in a real project ───
class AnagramScanner {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(AnagramScanner.class.getName());

    // Fixed-size window, frequency compare. O(26n) time, O(26) space.
    List<Integer> findAnagramStarts(String text, String pattern) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        int pLen = pattern.length();
        if (text.length() < pLen) return result;

        int[] need = new int[26];
        int[] window = new int[26];
        for (int i = 0; i < pLen; i++) need[pattern.charAt(i) - 'a']++;

        for (int r = 0; r < text.length(); r++) {
            window[text.charAt(r) - 'a']++;                 // add entering char
            if (r >= pLen) window[text.charAt(r - pLen) - 'a']--; // drop leaving char
            if (r >= pLen - 1 && Arrays.equals(window, need)) {
                result.add(r - pLen + 1);                   // window start index
            }
        }
        log.info(() -> "Found " + result.size() + " anagram windows");
        return result;
    }
}

// ─── Would be in BankLogFormatter.java in a real project ───
class BankLogFormatter extends java.util.logging.Formatter {
    @Override public String format(LogRecord r) {
        return String.format("%1$tF %1$tT %2$-7s %3$s%n",
                new java.util.Date(r.getMillis()), r.getLevel(), r.getMessage());
    }
}
